{"name":"Celestine","hex":"#85c1c4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Celestine","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of a Roman Catholic monastic order, a branch of the Benedictines, founded in 1244."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A unisex given name."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Celestine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pope Celestine I","blocks":[{"text":"Pope Celestine I was the bishop of Rome from 10 September 422 to his death on 27 July 432. Celestine's pontificate was largely spent combatting various teachings deemed heretical. He was instrumental in the condemnation of Nestorius in the Council of Ephesus when \nCyril of Alexandria appealed him to make a decision; in response, Celestine delegated to Cyril the job of condemning Nestorius if he did not recant his teachings. He supported the mission of the Gallic bishops that sent Germanus of Auxerre in 429, to Britain to address Pelagianism, and later commissioned Palladius as bishop to the Scots of Ireland and northern Britain."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Dundalk_Saint_Patrick%27s_Pro-Cathedral_West_Aisle_Window_06_Lower_Lights_2013_09_23%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Celestine_I","wikidata":"Q160922"},{"title":"Pope Celestine II","blocks":[{"text":"Pope Celestine II, born Guido di Castello, was head of the Catholic Church and ruler of the Papal States from 26 September 1143 to his death on 8 March 1144."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Pope_Celestine%2C_St_William_and_an_unidentified_Prelate%2C_East_Window%2C_York_Minster.jpg/330px-Pope_Celestine%2C_St_William_and_an_unidentified_Prelate%2C_East_Window%2C_York_Minster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Celestine_II","wikidata":"Q171144"},{"title":"Celestine (mineral)","blocks":[{"text":"Celestine (the IMA-accepted name) or celestite is a mineral consisting of strontium sulfate (SrSO4). The mineral is named after its occasional delicate blue color. Celestine and the carbonate mineral strontianite are the principal sources of the element strontium, commonly used in fireworks and in various metal alloys."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Celestine_-_Sakoany_deposit%2C_Katsepy%2C_Mitsinjo%2C_Boeny%2C_Madagascar.jpg/330px-Celestine_-_Sakoany_deposit%2C_Katsepy%2C_Mitsinjo%2C_Boeny%2C_Madagascar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Celestine_(mineral)","wikidata":"Q407221"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Patrick%27s_mission_to_Ireland_from_the_Pope%2C_Kilkenny_cathedral.jpg/500px-Patrick%27s_mission_to_Ireland_from_the_Pope%2C_Kilkenny_cathedral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Patrick%27s_mission_to_Ireland_from_the_Pope%2C_Kilkenny_cathedral.jpg/500px-Patrick%27s_mission_to_Ireland_from_the_Pope%2C_Kilkenny_cathedral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Saint Patrick sent to Ireland by Pope Celestine I; wall mosaic in St Mary's Cathedral, Kilkenny.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APatrick's_mission_to_Ireland_from_the_Pope%2C_Kilkenny_cathedral.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Caelestinus_II._podoba.jpg/330px-Caelestinus_II._podoba.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Caelestinus_II._podoba.jpg/330px-Caelestinus_II._podoba.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"19th-century religious card depicting Celest